#4471 was merged on subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) and
marked known as one issue. Read by tenant and by what the reports say, it holds three problems.
Each now has a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 closed on 5 October, but one of its three problems has no fix yet: the one-day move.
</Callout>

## Where they stand

| problem | outcome | ticket |
|---|---|---|
| `parser` | fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` | [#1](https://github.com/Urolas/support-desk-4471-run2/issues/1), closed |
| `hint` | won't fix: the booking-v3 form is retired | [#2](https://github.com/Urolas/support-desk-4471-run2/issues/2), closed |
| `one-day` | no fix: neither change moves a date by one day | [#3](https://github.com/Urolas/support-desk-4471-run2/issues/3), open |

## The three problems

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Day-first tenants get the day
  and month swapped: booked 2 December, invite says 12 February.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3's form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first tenants on
  that form type month first, as the hint asks, and the form reads it day first. Only form
  reports from tenants on booking-v3. The parser fix does not touch it.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, not by a swap. 12 May becomes 11 May; Lisbon gets
  Tuesday and Toronto gets Monday for the same meeting; an invite arrives before its booking.
  The desk noted these reports as one-day moves and counts them under `parser` by tenant; the
  table keeps that count and gives them their own row too. A swap cannot move a date by one day.
  This looks like a time zone problem, not yet confirmed.

## What intake guessed

Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser, not from the tenant. The tag is wrong for a
large share of the `hint` reports. Do not triage on it.

## The table this page replaces

The view on the [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471), built by tenant only:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A subject line says how a report reads, not what caused it. Read the tenant and the body
  before you merge.
- When a note says a report does not fit its cause, it may be a separate problem. Give it a
  row, not a footnote.

